module

-- **OPEN, but it should be FINISHED.** This file is *not* calculus-specific and
-- is not meant to be written per checker. It carries a `sorry` here only
-- because the template cannot yet ship the proof.
--
-- In Logos the corresponding file is byte-identical between `Cpc` (591 rules)
-- and `CpcMini` (5 rules) -- packages that differ in rule set, in signature and
-- in which invariants their rules need. It names no rule and no operator, and
-- uses three `Term` constructors. It is a proof about a stack machine that
-- pushes assumptions and proven facts, and about the invariants that machine
-- maintains.
--
-- So it should arrive complete, like `Api.lean` and `ApiChecks.lean` do. Two
-- things stand in the way, and neither is about this file:
--
--   * the checker layer it rests on -- roughly 5,000 lines -- is maintained
--     per package upstream rather than seeded into new ones;
--   * `Proofs/RuleLemmas.lean` imports every rule module, so this file cannot
--     *build* until every rule proof does. That is a build-order problem, not
--     an editing one, and the fix is to take the two rule-bridge theorems as
--     hypotheses rather than imports -- four call sites in the whole file.
--
-- Both are written up as item 5 of docs/eoc-requests.md in Eudaimonia, which is
-- the top of that list for exactly this reason.
--
-- **Do not start here.** What genuinely differs between two checkers is
-- `Proofs/Invariants/Extra.lean`, and that is where to look first.
--
-- **The theorem this checker is for.** Everything else exists to state it, to
-- compute it, or to connect it to what the executable prints.
--
-- It is a `sorry`. Discharging it is the work: `CheckerCore.lean` supplies the
-- rule-agnostic half, `RuleLemmas.lean` the per-rule half, and this is where
-- they are put together.
--
-- `ApiCorrect.lean` restates it about the text of a proof file, and does so by
-- *referring* to this theorem rather than reproving anything -- so filling this
-- in is what makes the executable\'s `correct` mean something, with no other
-- file needing to change.
--
-- In Logos: `Cpc/Proofs/Checker.lean`, whose `correct___eo_is_refutation` this
-- corresponds to.
public import @CALCULUS@.Proofs.RuleLemmas
import all @CALCULUS@.Proofs.RuleLemmas

public section

namespace Eo

/--
Soundness: a proof this checker accepts is a refutation of its assumptions.

If `pf` runs from the assumption chain `F` and reaches `false`, and `F` and `pf`
stay inside the fragment the SMT-LIB semantics models, then `F` is
unsatisfiable.

The two side conditions are not decoration. The rule proofs only ever interpret
a term through `__eo_to_smt`, so a proof mentioning something with no SMT-LIB
counterpart is outside what they establish. They are `Proofs/Assumptions.lean`,
which is also where the instances that decide them at run time come from.
-/
theorem correct___eo_is_refutation (F : Term) (pf : CCmdList) :
    TranslatableAssumptionList F ->
    CmdListTranslationOk pf ->
    eo_is_refutation F pf ->
    eo_satisfiability F false := by
  sorry

end Eo
